; regression test for a second, independent instance of the same eigenvariable-unification
; class of bug as forall-elim's (see doc/kurt-soundness.md's dedicated write-up, section 0b).
; found via a much narrower-looking report ("a declared-but-unconstrained `var` can get
; treated as boolean anyway"), which turned out to be one *symptom* of this bug, not its cause.
;
; root cause: `impl_elim`'s handling of an `iff`-shaped theory fact (e.g. `not-not`: `%A iff
; (not (not %A))`) has a "third attempt" that unifies the goal directly against the whole,
; unstripped fact, meant to catch a literal restatement -- but unlike the other two attempts
; (which recurse into the plain-implication branch, which *does* block the goal's own free
; variables from being assigned during matching), this third attempt used the caller's state
; completely unblocked. A goal's free variable (`a` below) is renamed to a fresh internal name
; before matching regardless of whether it's boolean, so it was free to be unified straight to
; `not (not C)`, satisfying `not-not`'s shape for *some* value of `a` -- never asserted to hold
; -- rather than being required to hold for whatever `a` actually, specifically is.
load prop
var a
bool C
expect "ProofError"
    a iff C

; confirms it's not specific to `var`'s boolean-inference: a bare schema variable used
; directly at top level (no `var` involved at all) hits the exact same bug
expect "ProofError"
    %A iff C
;;; Proof checked.
